// Generated by scripts/synastry/apply-overlays.py from docs/redesign/drafts.
// Edit the draft and rerun; do not edit by hand. Keys: planet, then the
// house (1-12) of the OTHER person's chart it falls in. Each text: hook,
// blank line, paragraphs. Sources at the top of each draft.

export const OVERLAY_TEXT: Record<string, Record<string, string>> = {
  "SUN": {
    "1": "The Sun person sees the other clearly, and the other shines more for it.\n\nThe Sun lands on the self here: looks, manner, temperament, the 1st house's whole territory. The other tends to feel more confident around the Sun person, and more like themselves.\n\nIt's a strong, visible bond, the kind friends notice. Lilly's Sun has \"a kind of itching desire to Rule and Sway where he comes,\" and the one caution lives there: the Sun person can start directing the other's image. Admiration works. Art direction, less so.",
    "2": "The Sun person shapes how the other earns, spends and values things.\n\nLilly's 2nd is money and possessions, and he notes the Sun there tends to scatter money. In synastry, the Sun person is generous with the other and encourages a bigger idea of what the other is worth.\n\nThat can mean a raise, a better rate, a sense that self worth and net worth are separate things. It can also mean expensive dinners nobody budgeted for. The healthy version is the Sun person helping the other value themselves, not the furniture.",
    "3": "Constant conversation, and the Sun person sets the topic.\n\nThe 3rd house is daily talk, messages, short trips and the neighborhood. With the Sun here, the Sun person becomes part of the other's everyday chatter, and much of what they talk about is the Sun person.\n\nThat's less vain than it sounds. These two think out loud together and run errands that turn into outings. If one person tends to talk and the other to listen, the 3rd is where it shows. Taking turns keeps it a conversation.",
    "4": "The Sun person feels like family, and may become it.\n\nHome, roots and the family a person comes from: that's the 4th. Lilly says the Sun here shows a father \"of a noble disposition.\" In synastry, the Sun person often feels protective, steady and a little parental in the other's private life.\n\nThat's comforting, and it's why this placement shows up in couples who set up house early. It can also stir old family patterns, since the Sun person is standing where the other's parents once stood. Most of the time the other is glad of the company.",
    "5": "Romance, fun and a lot of showing off for each other.\n\nThe 5th is the house of pleasure, play, flirting and children. The Sun here makes the Sun person the other's favorite source of fun, and often a real crush.\n\nThese two enjoy being seen enjoying themselves. Dates are events. It also makes this a common placement for couples who want children together. The only question is endurance: the 5th is a party, and parties need someone to do the dishes.",
    "6": "The Sun person shapes the other's daily routine, mostly for the better.\n\nThe 6th covers daily work, habits and the tasks of an ordinary week. With the Sun here, the Sun person brings purpose to the other's routines: a better schedule, more exercise, a job that finally makes sense.\n\nIt's practical rather than poetic. The care shows up as help. The catch: the Sun person may start managing the other's calendar, which is love until it becomes supervision. Asking first keeps it the former.",
    "7": "The Sun person looks like the partner the other was waiting for.\n\nLilly's 7th is marriage, partners and contracts. The Sun here ranks among the classic signs that the other sees the Sun person as a partner, and often as an equal across the table.\n\nPartnership is the point of this placement. Lilly also lists open enemies in the 7th, and the Sun person can be a strong opponent in an argument. Lilly's Sun is \"very faithful,\" though, and keeps promises to the minute, which is the part that tends to last.",
    "8": "Deep, private, and tangled up in shared money.\n\nThe 8th is other people's money: shared accounts, inheritances, debts and what two people owe each other. The Sun here makes the bond intense and private. The Sun person reaches parts of the other's life that usually stay closed.\n\nIt's rarely casual. These two tend to merge finances and secrets early. That closeness is the strength of the placement, and it's also why trust matters more than usual. Honesty about money is the simplest way to keep it.",
    "9": "The Sun person widens the other's world.\n\nLilly says the Sun rejoices in the 9th, the house of long journeys, faith and learning. In synastry, the Sun person opens the other to new places and new ideas, and often brings a different background or belief.\n\nTravel tends to be good for this pair, and so do big conversations. The Sun person can become a kind of teacher, which suits both people as long as the lessons go both ways now and then.",
    "10": "The Sun person raises the other's standing, in public and at work.\n\nLilly writes that the Sun does much to fortune the 10th, the house of reputation and rank. In synastry, the Sun person is an asset to the other's ambitions: an introduction, a push, a name that helps.\n\nThese are often power couples, or at least couples who are proud of each other in public. The thing to keep is a private life that doesn't depend on the other's career going well. Reputation is a fine thing to share and a poor thing to build a marriage on.",
    "11": "Best friends with shared plans, and a circle that welcomes the Sun person.\n\nThe 11th, Lilly's house of friendship and hope, has the Sun as its natural partner. Here the Sun person becomes one of the other's closest friends and a supporter of the other's long range plans.\n\nGroups go well for these two, and so do causes. It's a warm, easy placement that ages well, because friendship is what remains when the novelty wears off. In a romance, the only risk is that it stays a friendship longer than one of them would like.",
    "12": "The Sun person lights up what the other keeps hidden.\n\nThe 12th holds the private side: sorrows, fears and the ways people trip themselves up. With the Sun here, the Sun person sees what the other doesn't show the world, and tends to be generous about it.\n\nIt can be a healing bond, and sometimes a secret one. The care is real, and the other often feels understood in a way that's hard to explain. The pattern to watch is the Sun person playing rescuer. Light is welcome. A spotlight on every shadow is a lot."
  },
  "MOON": {
    "1": "The Moon person tunes in to the other at once.\n\nBody, mood and manner belong to the 1st house, and the Moon person is sensitive to all three. The Moon person can read the other's face before the other knows what it says.\n\nThat's comforting and occasionally unnerving. The other feels noticed and looked after. Lilly's Moon is \"a soft, tender creature,\" and the care here is soft too. The work is letting the other have a bad mood without the Moon person taking it home.",
    "2": "Comfort tied to security: food, money, the things that make a home.\n\nIn the 2nd, the house of money and possessions, the Moon person feels safe through the other's resources, and makes the other feel cared for in practical ways: a full fridge, a careful budget, a gift that was actually needed.\n\nSecurity is the love language of this placement. Its catch is that moods and money tangle. A tense week at work can feel like a threat to the relationship. Separating the two, at least in conversation, helps a lot.",
    "3": "Endless texts, daily updates, and comfort in small talk.\n\nLilly's 3rd house is the Moon's joy: \"much travel, seldom quiet.\" The Moon person feels at home in the other's everyday life of messages, errands and short trips.\n\nThese two talk about nothing for hours and both enjoy it. The relationship lives in the small stuff, which is where most relationships actually live. Lilly's Moon \"hunts for novelties,\" so variety keeps it fresh. A new route home counts.",
    "4": "The Moon person feels at home with the other, literally.\n\nThe 4th is home, family and roots. The Moon here is one of the strongest signs of domestic comfort in synastry: the Moon person feels safe in the other's private world, and the other feels like family.\n\nIt's a nesting placement. Shared meals, shared space, often an early move in together. The family question comes up too, since the Moon person may get pulled into the other's relatives' business. Usually the Moon person is happy to be.",
    "5": "Playful affection and an easy, childlike delight in each other.\n\nFun, romance, creativity and children: the 5th is the other's playground, and the Moon person relaxes in it. Dates feel like play, and play feels safe.\n\nThere's often a sweetness to this one. The Moon person enjoys the other's jokes, hobbies and enthusiasms. Lilly's Moon \"lives for the present,\" which suits the 5th perfectly. The planning can come later, and someone does have to make it come.",
    "6": "The Moon person cares through daily help, and needs routine to feel safe.\n\nThe 6th is daily work, habits and ordinary tasks. With the Moon here, the Moon person shows care through the routine: cooking, reminders, the small jobs that make a day go well.\n\nIt's a quiet kind of love, and the other may not notice it until it stops. The Moon person also needs the routine to stay steady, and feels unsettled when plans change. A thank you said out loud is the maintenance this placement asks for.",
    "7": "The Moon person feels like a natural partner.\n\nLilly's 7th is marriage and partners, and the Moon is its natural partner. Here the Moon person feels emotionally suited to the other, and the other often senses a fit early on.\n\nIt's a gentle, receptive placement. The Moon person brings sensitivity to the partnership and picks up on what the other needs. Moods do swing, since the Moon changes faster than any planet. Couples who last with this placement learn the rhythm instead of fighting it.",
    "8": "Strong feelings, deep trust, and the occasional tangle.\n\nThe 8th is shared money, inheritances and the closeness beyond polite. The Moon here pulls feelings deep. The Moon person reacts strongly to the other's private life, and the other lets the Moon person in further than most.\n\nIt's intense, protective and private. The risk is that feelings become possessive, or that money carries more emotion than it should. When trust is solid, this placement makes for a very loyal pair.",
    "9": "The Moon person finds comfort in the other's beliefs and adventures.\n\nLong journeys, faith and learning sit in the 9th. The Moon person feels at home in the other's big ideas, and eager to go along on the other's travels.\n\nLilly's Moon hunts for novelties and likes to move house, so this placement often comes with a passport. It also tends to soften differences in background or belief, since the Moon person finds the other's world appealing rather than strange.",
    "10": "The Moon person cares about the other's work and reputation.\n\nWith the Moon in the other's house of career and public life, the Moon person is emotionally invested in how the other does at work. Proud at the good news, worried at the bad.\n\nIt's supportive, and sometimes a little public: these two may be known as a pair in the other's professional world. The thing to watch is moods tied to the other's career. A bad review at work shouldn't feel like a bad week at home.",
    "11": "A comfortable friendship that feels like family.\n\nIn the 11th, Lilly's house of friends and hopes, the Moon person feels at ease in the other's circle and invested in the other's hopes.\n\nThese two are often each other's emotional safe harbor, a friend who happens to be more. Group settings go well, and so does long range planning. The Moon person needs to feel included in those plans, not just told about them.",
    "12": "A private bond with deep, unspoken feelings.\n\nPrivate sorrows and fears are the 12th house's business. With the Moon there, the Moon person feels the other's buried moods, sometimes before the other admits to having them.\n\nIt's tender and intuitive, and it can be heavy. The Moon person may absorb the other's worries without saying so. Talking about feelings out loud, even awkwardly, keeps this from turning into two people quietly carrying the same weight."
  },
  "MERCURY": {
    "1": "The Mercury person reads the other quickly, and says so.\n\nLilly says Mercury rejoices in the 1st, the house of the person themselves. In synastry, the Mercury person notices the other's habits, tells, and turns of phrase, and has a comment for most of them.\n\nIt makes for a lively start. The other feels seen, sometimes a little too accurately. Lilly's Mercury is \"sharp and witty,\" and the best of this placement is a running joke that lasts years. The care needed is in the delivery: observations land better as curiosity than as critique.",
    "2": "Talk about money, and good ideas for making it.\n\nMoney and possessions live in the 2nd. Mercury here makes the Mercury person a useful adviser on the other's finances: a better deal, a smarter purchase, a side business that started as a conversation over dinner.\n\nLilly calls Mercury \"unbeatable in trade,\" and this placement often shows up in couples who run something together. The friction, when it comes, is chatter about spending. Opinions on every receipt get old. A shared plan beats a running commentary.",
    "3": "Two people who never run out of things to say.\n\nTalk, letters, siblings and short trips: the 3rd is Mercury's natural ground, and the Mercury person fits right into it. These two message all day, finish each other's errands and can make a grocery run sound like an expedition.\n\nIt's one of the easiest placements for friendship, and it keeps romance light on its feet. The only thing it can't do is the quiet. Some evenings call for silence, and these two may need to schedule it.",
    "4": "Long talks at the kitchen table.\n\nThe 4th is home, family and roots, and Mercury brings conversation into it. The Mercury person is the one the other talks to about family history, the house, the plans for where to live.\n\nIt's a placement for couples who make decisions together and talk them through. It can also mean a busy home: books on every surface, a phone always ringing, relatives who drop in to talk. The Mercury person tends to enjoy all of it.",
    "5": "Wordplay, flirting and a shared sense of humor.\n\nFun, romance and play belong to the 5th. Mercury here makes the flirting verbal. The Mercury person makes the other laugh, and the jokes are a large part of the attraction.\n\nGames, quizzes and creative projects suit this pair. Lilly's Mercury searches into \"every mystery and branch of learning,\" so dates often double as lessons in something. If the romance needs more than wit, it usually finds it. The wit gets there first.",
    "6": "The Mercury person organizes the other's day.\n\nThe 6th is daily work and routine, and its natural partner is Mercury. Here the Mercury person helps the other plan, schedule and solve the small problems of an ordinary week.\n\nLists, reminders and shared calendars come easily to this pair, and work projects go well. The pitfall is nagging, since Mercury notices every loose end. When the help is offered rather than imposed, the other's life simply runs better.",
    "7": "Partners who talk things through, and negotiate everything.\n\nLanding in Lilly's house of marriage and contracts, Mercury makes the Mercury person feel like a natural conversation partner, and often a business one.\n\nThese two discuss before they decide. They're good at agreements, and can argue a point for an hour without either one minding. The 7th also covers open disputes, and Lilly's Mercury is \"an excellent debater,\" so winning can become the hobby. Agreeing on what they agree on keeps it friendly.",
    "8": "Deep conversations, and frank talk about money and secrets.\n\nThe 8th covers shared money, debts and the private things people rarely say. Mercury here gets the conversation there. The Mercury person asks the questions others are too polite to ask, and the other answers.\n\nThat's valuable: these two can discuss wills, debts and old wounds without flinching. It can also feel like an interview. Curiosity is a kindness only when the other is ready, so timing matters here more than anywhere.",
    "9": "A meeting of minds about the big questions.\n\nLilly's 9th covers religion, long journeys and learning. With Mercury here, the Mercury person becomes the other's favorite person to think with: about faith, politics, travel plans and books.\n\nThis is a placement for study partners and travel companions. Lilly's Mercury \"loves travel\" and learns \"almost any thing without a Teacher,\" so these two often teach themselves something together. Disagreements stay mostly intellectual, which is the most fun kind.",
    "10": "The Mercury person helps with the other's career, in words.\n\nCareer, reputation and public standing: that's the 10th. Mercury here makes the Mercury person the other's editor, speechwriter or sounding board at work. Resumes get better. Pitches get sharper.\n\nIt's a practical, useful placement. Couples who work in the same field often have it. The one caution is advice the other didn't ask for, since Mercury has thoughts on everything the other does in public. Most of the thoughts are good, which makes them harder to ignore.",
    "11": "Plans, group chats and friends in common.\n\nFriends, hopes and allies fill the 11th. Mercury here plugs the Mercury person into the other's social circle and long range plans. These two share friends quickly and tend to talk about the future a lot.\n\nThe group chat may be their natural habitat. The Mercury person is good at connecting the other with people who can help. It's an easy, sociable placement that works as well for friendship as for romance.",
    "12": "The Mercury person hears what the other doesn't say.\n\nHidden worries and private thoughts are 12th house material. Mercury here gives the Mercury person a knack for reading between the other's lines. The other may find they confide things they've never said aloud.\n\nThat makes for a trusted listener. It can also go the other way, with conversations that circle around a subject neither one names. Lilly's Mercury takes on the nature of what it touches, \"with the good he is good,\" so the tone of this one depends on the kindness both bring."
  },
  "VENUS": {
    "1": "The Venus person likes the other on sight, and it shows.\n\nThe 1st house is the person: body, face, manner, the way someone walks into a room. Venus landing there means the Venus person is drawn to exactly those things. Not the résumé, not the potential. The person.\n\nThat's flattering to receive and easy to trust, because it isn't conditional on anything. The other tends to feel more attractive around the Venus person, and often dresses the part. The risk is small and mostly comic: the Venus person may find it hard to stay annoyed at a face they enjoy this much.",
    "2": "Affection with a practical streak: the Venus person adds to what the other has.\n\nLilly's 2nd house is money and movable goods, the things a person owns and earns. Venus here is generous. Gifts, good taste in what to buy, a habit of improving the other's finances or at least their furniture.\n\nIt also tends to make the other feel valued in the literal sense. The one thing to watch is keeping score. Venus gives freely, and the other can come to expect the giving without noticing it, which is how a pleasant contact turns into an invisible job.",
    "3": "Easy talk, constant messages, and a friendship inside the romance.\n\nThe 3rd house covers daily talk, letters, short trips and the people next door. Venus here makes conversation the main pleasure. These two text more than they need to, and their best dates are errands.\n\nPairs with this placement often say they were friends first, or that they still are. It's light rather than intense, and that's the point. A relationship that enjoys a trip to the hardware store has solved most of the problems other couples spend years on.",
    "4": "The Venus person makes the other's home feel like home.\n\nHome is the 4th house in the plainest sense: land, family roots and, in Lilly's words, \"the end of any matter.\" Venus here brings comfort to the private side of the other's life. The Venus person is good in the kitchen, or at least good company in it.\n\nThis is one of the classic marks of a couple who nest. The other feels safe enough to be unimpressive, which is underrated. If there's friction, it's over family: the Venus person may be asked to love the other's relatives too, and Venus, being Venus, usually manages.",
    "5": "Fun, flirting and romance, in the house built for them.\n\nLilly calls the 5th \"the house of Pleasure, Delight and Merriment\" and says Venus rejoices there. It's the house of dates, parties, play and children. Venus landing in it is about as natural a fit as astrology offers.\n\nThe relationship is enjoyable, and both people know it. The question this contact doesn't answer is whether the fun has a second act. Pleasure is a fine foundation; it just isn't a whole house. Couples who last with it add the dull parts on purpose.",
    "6": "Love shown through help: chores, care and showing up.\n\nThe 6th is daily work, routine and service. Venus here doesn't arrive with roses. It arrives with soup, a ride to the appointment and an offer to handle the paperwork.\n\nThat can look unromantic from outside and feel deeply romantic from inside. The other's ordinary days get easier, which is what most people actually want from love. The risk is that the Venus person becomes staff. Thanks said out loud keep this one sweet.",
    "7": "The Venus person looks like a partner, to both of them.\n\nLilly's 7th house is marriage, partners and contracts. Venus here is one of the plainest signs in synastry that the other sees the Venus person as partner material, often early and sometimes before either says so.\n\nIt doesn't guarantee a wedding; it guarantees the thought. The 7th is also the house of open enemies, which is Lilly's reminder that partners are the people who get to argue with us directly. Venus softens that side well. Disagreements here tend to end in compromise rather than a scoreboard.",
    "8": "Attraction with depth, and money that gets mixed.\n\nThe 8th is other people's money: shared accounts, inheritances, debts and gifts that come with strings. Venus here draws the two into shared resources faster than most couples, and into a closeness that goes past the polite stage.\n\nIt's intense in a quiet way. The Venus person sees what the other doesn't usually show, and likes it anyway. That's the gift. The work is keeping the money honest, because money and closeness tangle easily, and untangling them later is nobody's idea of romance.",
    "9": "The Venus person opens the other's world: travel, ideas, belief.\n\nLong journeys, religion, learning and the family one marries into: that's Lilly's 9th house. Venus here makes the relationship feel like an education the other actually enjoys. These two plan trips, swap books and argue happily about large questions.\n\nThe Venus person often brings a different culture, faith or background, or simply a wider view, and the other finds it attractive rather than threatening. The other's family tends to like this one too, which is not nothing.",
    "10": "The Venus person admires the other's work, and helps it along.\n\nThe 10th is career, honor and public standing. Venus here likes the other in public: proud of their work, good at their events, and often a real asset to their reputation.\n\nIt suits couples who are seen together, and it can blur into a working partnership. The thing to protect is the private side. Admiration for what someone does is lovely, as long as it isn't the only thing being admired.",
    "11": "Friends first, friends always, and a shared set of hopes.\n\nFriends, hopes and allies fill the 11th. Venus here makes the Venus person the other's favorite friend, and usually welcome in their circle from the start.\n\nThe relationship rests on liking more than longing, which ages well. It also tends to be generous about the other's plans: the Venus person wants the other's hopes to come true and says so. If romance is the goal, the only risk is staying friends a little too comfortably for a little too long.",
    "12": "A private, protective love, kept a little out of sight.\n\nThe 12th is what's hidden: private sorrows, secret enemies and the ways people undo themselves. Venus here gets in behind the other's defenses. The Venus person sees the parts the other keeps from the world, and is kind about them.\n\nIt's tender, and it can be secret, either because the relationship itself is kept quiet or because so much of it happens where nobody else is looking. The care is real. The pattern to avoid is the Venus person rescuing and the other being rescued, indefinitely."
  },
  "MARS": {
    "1": "The Mars person fires up the other, in every sense.\n\nThe 1st house is the person. Mars landing there acts on the other directly: energy, nerve, attraction and, now and then, irritation. Lilly's Mars is bold and \"impatient of servitude,\" and the other tends to catch some of that boldness.\n\nThis is a strong contact for chemistry and for getting each other moving. It's also the one where small things start fights, because Mars is pressing on the other's actual self. Good pairs with it treat the arguments as weather. They pass, and the energy stays.",
    "2": "Drive aimed at money: the Mars person spurs the other to earn, or to spend.\n\nLilly's 2nd house is money and goods, and he says Mars there tends to scatter them. In synastry, the Mars person stirs up the other's finances, for better or worse. Bold purchases, side ventures, sudden plans.\n\nWhen it works, the Mars person is the push the other needed to ask for a raise. When it doesn't, the two disagree about money with real heat. A shared budget isn't romantic, but with this contact it's cheaper than the alternative.",
    "3": "Quick talk, sharp banter, and the occasional sharp word.\n\nThe 3rd is talk, letters and short trips, and Lilly says Mars there is \"not too bad.\" That's fair. The Mars person makes conversations lively, opinions stronger and plans happen faster.\n\nDebate is a sport for these two. It's fun until it isn't, and the line is usually tone rather than topic. The upside is a couple that never runs out of things to say and almost never sulks, since everything gets said at the time.",
    "4": "The Mars person stirs up the other's home life.\n\nMars in the 4th, the house of home and roots, brings energy into the other's home life: renovation projects, moving house, rearranging the furniture at midnight. It also brings heat to family matters.\n\nLiving together is where this contact shows. The Mars person wants things done, and the other's home is where they get done. That can be a gift or an invasion, depending on whether the other asked. Agreeing early on whose space is whose saves a great deal of noise.",
    "5": "Strong chemistry and a competitive streak, even about fun.\n\nThe 5th is pleasure, play and romance. Lilly counts Mars as bad there, since it's the house of Venus's delight and Mars is not delicate. In synastry, that reads as heat. Attraction is high, play gets physical, and games get competitive.\n\nThese two have a good time, and both want to win it. The danger is that pleasure becomes a contest, and that someone keeps score of who planned the last date. Keep the competition in the game, not in the relationship, and it stays a very lively pairing.",
    "6": "The Mars person gets the other's work done, and expects the same.\n\nLilly says Mars rejoices in the 6th, the house of daily work and routine. In synastry, the Mars person shakes up the other's habits: workouts, schedules, the projects that were stuck. It works well for couples who work together.\n\nThe flip side is supervision. Mars in someone's routine can start to feel like a coach who didn't sign up to be hired. When the Mars person helps by doing rather than directing, this is one of the most useful contacts there is.",
    "7": "Attraction and argument both happen in the open.\n\nLilly's 7th house is marriage and partners, and also open enemies. He lists Mars there as unfortunate for marriage. In synastry, that becomes a partner who brings strong desire and strong opinions into the relationship itself.\n\nIt's not a doom placement. It's an honest one. These two don't hide disagreements; they have them, out loud. Couples who last with this contact learn to fight fair, and many say the sparring is part of what keeps the attraction alive.",
    "8": "Intense attraction, and strong feelings about shared money.\n\nThe 8th covers shared money, debts and inheritances, and the closeness that goes past polite. Mars here brings intensity. Attraction runs deep, and so do disagreements about what belongs to whom.\n\nThis is the contact for a relationship that is never lukewarm. It's also one where trust has to be earned and kept, because the Mars person reaches places the other doesn't usually let anyone near. Clear agreements about money remove half the trouble before it starts.",
    "9": "The Mars person dares the other to go farther: travel, study, belief.\n\nLilly's 9th house is long journeys, religion and learning. Mars here is restless energy aimed at the big picture. These two book trips on impulse, take up subjects together and argue about beliefs with real conviction.\n\nIt's bracing. The other grows, sometimes faster than planned. The friction comes when Mars's convictions meet the other's: politics and religion are not side topics for this pair. Agreeing to disagree is a skill they'll use often.",
    "10": "The Mars person drives the other's ambition.\n\nThe 10th is career, honor and public standing. Mars here is a motivator, steering the other toward bigger goals, defends their reputation and has opinions about their boss.\n\nFor an ambitious pair, this is fuel. The risk is that the other feels managed rather than supported, or that the two compete in public. When the Mars person stays on the other's side rather than in their lane, careers tend to move.",
    "11": "The Mars person energizes the other's plans and friendships.\n\nLilly's 11th is friends, hopes and allies. Mars here gets the other's projects off the ground and livens up their social life: group trips, causes, things done with a crowd.\n\nIt can also mean friction with the other's friends, since Mars has opinions about them, too. Usually this pair works best as a team aimed at a shared goal. Given something to build, they build it.",
    "12": "Hidden friction, or a protector who works out of sight.\n\nThe 12th is what's hidden: private enemies, sorrow and self undoing. Mars here can play out two ways. The Mars person may stir up the other's private fears without meaning to, or may become the one who quietly defends them against everything.\n\nOften it's both. Anger here tends to go unspoken, which is the part to watch, since unspoken anger in the 12th has a long memory. Pairs who say things plainly turn this into fierce loyalty, and that's worth having."
  },
  "JUPITER": {
    "1": "The Jupiter person brings out the best in the other.\n\nThe 1st is the self, and Jupiter, Lilly's \"greater Fortune,\" is good news wherever it lands. The other tends to feel more confident, more generous and more hopeful around the Jupiter person.\n\nIt's an encouraging, warm placement. The Jupiter person believes in the other, sometimes more than the other does. If there's a catch, it's indulgence. Jupiter would rather praise than correct, and now and then the other needs the correction.",
    "2": "Generosity, and often a little more money.\n\nLilly writes that Jupiter in the 2nd is \"an argument of an estate,\" and in synastry the Jupiter person often improves the other's finances: good advice, good luck, sometimes plain generosity.\n\nThis is a comfortable placement. The other feels provided for and rarely worries about money with the Jupiter person around. Jupiter's weakness, in Lilly's words, is letting \"everyone cheat him,\" so a little care with lending keeps the generosity in the family.",
    "3": "Warm conversation, good news and easy days.\n\nThe 3rd is talk, siblings and short trips. Jupiter here makes the everyday friendly. The Jupiter person brings optimism to the other's routine conversations and tends to be well liked by the other's brothers, sisters and neighbors.\n\nRoad trips go well. So does small talk, which with Jupiter rarely stays small for long. It's an easy placement to live with, and a good one for friendship that stays kind when the romance has quiet weeks.",
    "4": "A generous presence in the other's home and family.\n\nHome and family belong to the 4th, and Jupiter here makes the Jupiter person a blessing to that side of the other's life. Bigger house, better holidays, family who approve.\n\nLilly's Jupiter is \"indulgent to spouse and children,\" and this placement often marks a couple who build a warm, open home. The only risk is excess: one more room, one more guest, one more dessert. Most people living in this placement don't count that as a risk.",
    "5": "Joy, play and a lot of good times.\n\nThe 5th is pleasure, romance and children, and Jupiter here enlarges all three. The Jupiter person makes life more fun for the other: more trips, more celebrations, more laughter.\n\nIt's one of the luckiest romance placements and a common one in couples with big families. Fun is easy here. Restraint is the harder part, and the bill for the fun has to land somewhere. Someone should keep the receipts.",
    "6": "The Jupiter person makes the other's daily grind lighter.\n\nJupiter among the other's chores and routines brings help and good humor to the other's ordinary days. The Jupiter person encourages better habits, better jobs and more patience with both.\n\nIt's a quietly lucky placement. Work problems shrink when the Jupiter person weighs in, partly through good advice and partly through Jupiter's habit of assuming things will work out. Usually they do.",
    "7": "A generous, fair minded partner.\n\nLilly's 7th is marriage and contracts, and his Jupiter \"loves fair dealing.\" Here the Jupiter person feels like a partner who will treat the other well, and usually does.\n\nThis is one of the best placements for a lasting partnership. Disagreements resolve fairly. Contracts, literal or not, are honored. The only weakness is that Jupiter avoids hard conversations in the name of peace. A partner willing to be blunt now and then makes this placement even better.",
    "8": "Shared money tends to grow, and trust comes easily.\n\nThe 8th is shared money, inheritances and what two people owe each other. Jupiter here is a good sign for joint finances: loans that work out, gifts that arrive on time, a partner who is generous with what's shared.\n\nIt also makes deep matters less frightening. The Jupiter person brings perspective and calm to the other's crises. Lilly's Jupiter is \"faithful,\" and this placement tends to make that the thing the other remembers most.",
    "9": "Travel, learning and shared beliefs.\n\nThe 9th is Jupiter's natural partner in Lilly's scheme, the house of long journeys, religion and study. Here the Jupiter person broadens the other's world in a way that feels like a gift.\n\nThis pair travels well, studies well and often shares a faith or philosophy, or builds one together. Lilly writes that Jupiter here makes a person devout or \"modestly given.\" The worst it does is an overstuffed itinerary.",
    "10": "The Jupiter person is good for the other's career.\n\nLilly names Jupiter among the planets that \"doe much Fortunate\" the 10th, the house of honor and profession. In synastry, the Jupiter person tends to open doors for the other: introductions, references, a timely word with the right person.\n\nIt suits couples whose careers grow together. The other's reputation usually improves with the Jupiter person at their side. The one caution is borrowed status. The Jupiter person's luck helps most when the other's work can stand on its own.",
    "11": "Friendship, shared hopes and a lucky social life.\n\nLilly writes that Jupiter \"doth especially rejoyce\" in the 11th, the house of friends and hopes. In synastry, it's one of the warmest placements there is. The Jupiter person is a true friend to the other and a backer of the other's dreams.\n\nThese two have good friends in common and a habit of making plans that come true. If they're a couple, the friendship is the foundation. If they're friends, they may well be friends for life.",
    "12": "A quiet guardian for the other's private troubles.\n\nThe 12th is what's hidden: sorrows, fears and self undoing. Jupiter here brings comfort to exactly those places. The Jupiter person helps the other through bad stretches, often without fanfare.\n\nIt's a protective, healing placement. Much of what the Jupiter person gives here goes unseen, and the Jupiter person rarely needs credit. Lilly's Jupiter is \"bashful\" as well as generous, and that's how this one feels: kind, without making a show of it."
  },
  "SATURN": {
    "1": "The Saturn person takes the other seriously, sometimes too seriously.\n\nThe 1st house is the self. Saturn here weighs on it. The Saturn person brings structure, steadiness and a sense of responsibility, and the other may feel both supported and watched.\n\nLilly's Saturn is \"patient in labor\" and \"reserved in words,\" and the best of this placement is a partner who helps the other grow up in good ways. The hard part is criticism, spoken or not. When the Saturn person shows approval openly, the other relaxes, and the bond gets very solid.",
    "2": "Careful money, and a partner who means it.\n\nMoney and goods belong to the 2nd. Saturn here makes the Saturn person a careful influence on the other's finances: budgets, savings, the long view. Lilly's Saturn is \"thrifty\" and careful \"about getting the goods of life.\"\n\nIt can feel restrictive, as if every purchase needs a reason. It can also build real security over time, which is Saturn's specialty. Agreeing on what money is for, not just how much there is, takes the chill out of this one.",
    "3": "Serious talk, few wasted words.\n\nThe 3rd is daily talk and short trips. Saturn here slows the conversation down. The Saturn person speaks carefully with the other and expects the same, and messages may be shorter than the other would like.\n\nWhat's said tends to be meant, though. Lilly's Saturn is \"reserved in words\" and \"grave in argument.\" This pair may not chat much, but they rarely say things they need to take back. Learning each other's pace solves most of it.",
    "4": "The Saturn person builds a lasting home with the other.\n\nHome and roots are the 4th, and Saturn here brings duty to them. The Saturn person feels responsible for the other's household: the repairs, the mortgage, the family obligations nobody else will take.\n\nIt's a heavy placement and a durable one. Couples with it tend to own property, stay put and take care of aging parents. The weight can feel like coldness at home. Warmth has to be added on purpose, and usually is.",
    "5": "Fun gets organized, and love gets tested.\n\nLilly counts Saturn as bad in the 5th, the house of pleasure and play. In synastry, the Saturn person can dampen the other's fun, or simply take it seriously: planned dates, careful spending, a long view of romance.\n\nThat isn't always a loss. Some people want a partner who takes the fun seriously. Still, the other may feel that spontaneity needs permission. A Saturn person who joins the play instead of supervising it turns this placement into lasting affection.",
    "6": "Two people who work well, and hard, together.\n\nWith Saturn among the other's daily routines, it makes the Saturn person a steady presence in the other's working life. Schedules hold. Projects finish. Bad habits get dropped.\n\nIt's productive and sometimes strict, with a faint sense that someone is keeping score on chores. Lilly's Saturn is \"hardworking,\" and this is the placement where that shows most. Credit given out loud keeps it a partnership and not a job.",
    "7": "A serious commitment, with real weight to it.\n\nLilly lists Saturn in the 7th as unfortunate for marriage, and older astrology treats it with caution. In practice, Saturn here often marks a relationship both people take very seriously: long engagements, formal commitments, partners who stay.\n\nThe weight is the point and also the difficulty. The Saturn person can seem cautious, or slow to show feeling. Couples who last with this placement tend to describe it as a relationship that grew stronger by being tested.",
    "8": "Trust earned slowly, and shared money handled carefully.\n\nThe 8th is shared money, debts and what's private, and its natural partner in Lilly's scheme is Saturn. Here the Saturn person is careful with what the two share: joint accounts, obligations, the other's secrets.\n\nTrust comes slowly, which is both the strength and the strain. Once given, it holds. Fears about money or loss can surface, and talking about them plainly is the cure. This is a placement for people who plan to be together a long time.",
    "9": "Serious about beliefs, and careful about the big picture.\n\nLong journeys, religion and learning are the 9th's concerns. Saturn here makes the Saturn person a sober influence on the other's beliefs and plans: questioning, testing, sometimes doubting.\n\nIt can feel like a wet blanket over the other's enthusiasms. It can also give them backbone. Ideas that survive the Saturn person's questions tend to be good ones. Travel happens, though likely with a spreadsheet.",
    "10": "The Saturn person shapes the other's career, through expectation.\n\nLilly writes that Saturn in the 10th limits honor. In synastry, the Saturn person can feel like a demanding boss in the other's working life: high standards, few compliments, a long view of success.\n\nThat pressure works for some people. Many careers have been built by someone who said \"not good enough yet.\" The trick is for the Saturn person to say \"good\" as well, often enough that the other believes it.",
    "11": "Loyal friends with a long shared plan.\n\nThe 11th is friends and hopes. Saturn here makes the Saturn person a slow but loyal friend, and a realist about the other's dreams. The Saturn person may be cautious about the other's social circle, and selective about it.\n\nIt's not a placement for big parties. It is a placement for long term goals reached together, because Saturn finishes what it starts. The other's hopes get trimmed to size, and then, often, met.",
    "12": "Private burdens, shared quietly.\n\nLilly says Saturn rejoices in the 12th, the house of hidden troubles. In synastry, the Saturn person can touch the other's private fears, sometimes by bringing them up and sometimes by standing guard over them.\n\nIt's a serious, private placement. The other may feel the Saturn person understands their worst days, or worries that the Saturn person is quietly judging. Plain speech helps here more than anywhere. What stays unsaid in the 12th tends to grow."
  }
}
